`ctlv_blob_member()` maps attr IDs to an enum whose numeric order is documented as "the order the root family declares them". That is a coupling to the generated `enum _ctlv_attr_blob` (TYPE=1..LENGTH=5), not enforced by anything. A `BUILD_BUG_ON` or building the table from the generated IDs would be more robust. Returning int with -1 for "none" is also awkward next to the `u32` `BIT()` uses; a bool plus out param or a table lookup would be simpler.

> +	switch (attr_id) {
> +	case CTLV_ATTR_BLOB_TYPE:
> +		return CTLV_BLOB_MEMBER_TYPE;
> +	case CTLV_ATTR_BLOB_ADDRESS:
> +		return CTLV_BLOB_MEMBER_ADDRESS;
> +	case CTLV_ATTR_BLOB_FD:
> +		return CTLV_BLOB_MEMBER_FD;
> +	case CTLV_ATTR_BLOB_OFFSET:
> +		return CTLV_BLOB_MEMBER_OFFSET;
> +	case CTLV_ATTR_BLOB_LENGTH:
> +		return CTLV_BLOB_MEMBER_LENGTH;
> +	default:
> +		return -1;
> +	}
> +}
> +
> +u32 ctlv_blob_desc_read(const struct ctlv_attr *blob, struct ctlv_blob_desc *desc,
> +			const struct ctlv_attr **bad)
> +{
> +	const struct ctlv_attr *child = NULL;
> +	enum ctlv_message_step step;
> +
> +	*desc = (struct ctlv_blob_desc){};
> +	while ((step = ctlv_message_next(blob, &child)) == CTLV_MESSAGE_STEP_ATTR) {
> +		int member = ctlv_blob_member(child->attr_id);
> +
> +		*bad = child;
> +		if (child->flags & ~CTLV_ATTR_F_NESTED)

Flag handling is inconsistent with `schema.c`: for ordinary attrs the request path accepts `CTLV_ATTR_F_IGNORE_IF_UNKNOWN` on a known attr (`CTLV_SCHEMA_ATTR_F_REQUEST`), whereas here any flag other than NESTED, including `IGNORE_IF_UNKNOWN`, is rejected as `UNKNOWN_FLAG`. Also, a child with an unknown flag and an unknown ID reports `UNKNOWN_FLAG` before `UNKNOWN_ATTR`; fine, but pick one documented order.

> +			return CTLV_VALIDATION_REASON_UNKNOWN_FLAG;
> +		if (child->flags & CTLV_ATTR_F_NESTED)
> +			return CTLV_VALIDATION_REASON_MALFORMED_MESSAGE;
> +		if (member < 0)
> +			return CTLV_VALIDATION_REASON_UNKNOWN_ATTR;
> +		if (ctlv_message_attr_payload_len(child) != sizeof(u64))
> +			return CTLV_VALIDATION_REASON_BAD_LENGTH;
> +		if (desc->present & BIT(member))

`CTLV_VALIDATION_REASON_BAD_BLOB_DESCRIPTOR` is returned for a duplicate member, but *bad points at the duplicate, while a missing member (see `ctlv_blob_variant`) leaves bad `NULL` so the offset lands on the blob nest. Reasonable, but nothing documents it; there is no kernel-doc at all on the two exported functions (return value is a `CTLV_VALIDATION_REASON_*` `u32` rather than an errno, which is unusual and easy to misuse, e.g. `if (ret < 0)`).

> +			return CTLV_VALIDATION_REASON_BAD_BLOB_DESCRIPTOR;
> +		desc->present |= BIT(member);
> +		desc->member[member] = *(const u64 *)ctlv_message_attr_payload(child);

The payload is read as `*(const u64 *)`. It is naturally 8-byte aligned only because the attr header is 16 bytes and the message base is aligned; a comment (as `schema.c` has in `ctlv_schema_scalar_value()`) or `get_unaligned()`/memcpy would document that. Also a third copy of the same read idiom (`schema.c` has `ctlv_schema_scalar_value()`); please share one helper in `message.h`.

> +	}
> +	*bad = NULL;

On `STEP_BAD` the loop leaves *bad as set for the previous attr and then it is reset to `NULL` below, so a caller can never learn which attr was truncated. That is OK for `schema.c` (it reports the nest), but then the 'bad' contract is just "the attr that failed a member check". Document it.

> +	if (step == CTLV_MESSAGE_STEP_BAD)
> +		return CTLV_VALIDATION_REASON_MALFORMED_MESSAGE;
> +	return 0;
> +}
> +
> +static u32 ctlv_blob_variant(const struct ctlv_blob_desc *desc)

`ctlv_blob_variant()` is a one-case switch with a default that rejects: speculative generality for backings (FD, OFFSET) that do not exist yet. `CTLV_BLOB_MEMBER_FD` and `CTLV_BLOB_MEMBER_OFFSET`, the fd/offset arm in `ctlv_blob_desc_check()` below, and the "4 members" case in `schema.c`'s max-length computation are all unreachable today (`CTLV_BLOB_TYPE_VA` is the only enum value in `ctlv_gen.h`). Either drop them until FD blobs land, or add the FD type in this series. Also, the helper does two things (type-specific shape check and returns a reason) and the name 'variant' does not tell that.

> +{
> +	u32 required = BIT(CTLV_BLOB_MEMBER_TYPE) | BIT(CTLV_BLOB_MEMBER_LENGTH);
> +
> +	switch (desc->member[CTLV_BLOB_MEMBER_TYPE]) {
> +	case CTLV_BLOB_TYPE_VA:
> +		required |= BIT(CTLV_BLOB_MEMBER_ADDRESS);
> +		break;
> +	default:
> +		return CTLV_VALIDATION_REASON_BAD_BLOB_DESCRIPTOR;
> +	}
> +	if (desc->present != required)
> +		return CTLV_VALIDATION_REASON_BAD_BLOB_DESCRIPTOR;
> +	return 0;
> +}
> +
> +u32 ctlv_blob_desc_check(const struct ctlv_blob_desc *desc, const struct ctlv_node_schema *node)
> +{
> +	u64 start, length, end;
> +	u32 reason;
> +
> +	reason = ctlv_blob_variant(desc);
> +	if (reason)
> +		return reason;
> +	if (!(node->blob.backings & BIT(desc->member[CTLV_BLOB_MEMBER_TYPE])))

Because `ctlv_blob_variant()` already restricted the type to VA, BIT(type) is safe here; but once more types are added, type is an arbitrary `u64` from userspace and BIT(type) with type >= 64 is UB/shift out of range. The ordering dependency (variant must reject unknown types first) is subtle; add a comment or guard `type < BITS_PER_TYPE(node->blob.backings)`.

> +		return CTLV_VALIDATION_REASON_BAD_BLOB_DESCRIPTOR;
> +
> +	length = desc->member[CTLV_BLOB_MEMBER_LENGTH];
> +	if (!length || length > node->blob.max_len)
> +		return CTLV_VALIDATION_REASON_BAD_BLOB_DESCRIPTOR;
> +	start = desc->member[desc->present & BIT(CTLV_BLOB_MEMBER_ADDRESS) ?

This ternary selects OFFSET when ADDRESS is absent, but `ctlv_blob_variant()` guarantees ADDRESS is present for the only accepted type, so the OFFSET branch is dead code today. Likewise `end` is computed but never used: `check_add_overflow()` is being used only for its flag, so `end` is a write-only local (consider `check_add_overflow()` into a dummy, or `if (length > U64_MAX - start)`).

> +				     CTLV_BLOB_MEMBER_ADDRESS :
> +				     CTLV_BLOB_MEMBER_OFFSET];
> +	if (!IS_ALIGNED(start, node->blob.alignment) || check_add_overflow(start, length, &end))

`IS_ALIGNED()` with alignment == 0 evaluates `(start & (0 - 1)) == 0`, i.e. requires start == 0; and with a non-power-of-two alignment it is meaningless. Nothing in this patch states the schema guarantees alignment is a non-zero power of two. For VA blobs a generator-emitted alignment of 0 (unspecified) would make every non-`NULL` address fail with `BAD_BLOB_DESCRIPTOR`. Please confirm the generator always emits >= 1, or treat 0 as 1. Also, only start+length overflow of `u64` is checked: there is no upper bound on a user VA (`TASK_SIZE` / `access_ok`), so the 'VA checks' promised in the commit message are only partly there.

> +		return CTLV_VALIDATION_REASON_BAD_BLOB_DESCRIPTOR;
> +	return 0;
> +}
